;;; A premise whose relation is a parameter its activator binds to `eq` scans
;;; stored facts — M1f **W52**, register row `W52/relvar-eq-premise`
;;; (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), filed by S1f.5.7 (T1f.5.7.1, 2026-11-09)
;;; from its step 1's probes, `open` and owned by S1f.5.7, on the user's
;;; ruling of 2026-11-07 (Q-M1e2.26: the reading holds wherever a relation can
;;; be written).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two facts `(p a)` and `(p b)`, and a rule whose third premise's relation
;;; is its parameter `?R`, activated by `(r eq)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Under `(r eq)` the third premise is `(eq ?x ?y)`, the relation of
;;; equality, whose facts are computed: the rule fires on `(a, a)` and `(b,
;;; b)`, so root holds `(hit a a)` and `(hit b b)`. H is `(hit a b)` and `(hit
;;; b a)`, which no rule refutes, so R is four states, each holding root's two.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The premise is a pattern over stored `eq` facts, which no state holds: the
;;; rule never fires, root holds no `hit`, and R is four states, none holding
;;; `(hit a a)` or `(hit b b)`, exit 0 (`ein solve FILE` and `ein oracle FILE`
;;; at `dc4c0bdc`, release and debug alike).

(relation p Node :why "{?1} is a node")
(relation hit Node Node :why "{?1} and {?2} are two nodes")
(p a)
(p b)
(rule r (?R)
  :match (and (p ?x) (p ?y) (?R ?x ?y))
  :assert (hit ?x ?y)
  :why "{?x} and {?y} are nodes in the relation {?R}")
(r eq)
